--------+--------------+--------------+-------------------+------------+------------+-------------------+--------------+--------------+\n52\t *\n53\t */\n54\t\n55\t/* double log1p(double x)\n56\t *\n57\t * Method :\n58\t *   1. Argument Reduction: find k and f such that\n59\t *            1+x = 2^k * (1+f),\n60\t *       where  sqrt(2)/2 < 1+f < sqrt(2) .\n61\t *\n62\t *      Note. If k=0, then f=x is exact. However, if k!=0, then f\n63\t *    may not be representable exactly. In that case, a correction\n64\t *    term is need. Let u=1+x rounded. Let c = (1+x)-u, then\n65\t *    log(1+x) - log(u) ~ c/u. Thus, we proceed to compute log(u),\n66\t *    and add back the correction term c/u.\n67\t *    (Note: when x > 2**53, one can simply return log(x))\n68\t *\n69\t *   2. Approximation of log1p(f).\n70\t *    Let s = f/(2+f) ; based on log(1+f) = log(1+s) - log(1-s)\n71\t *         = 2s + 2/3 s**3 + 2/5 s**5 + .....,\n72\t *              = 2s + s*R\n73\t *      We use a special Remez algorithm on [0,0.1716] to generate\n74\t *     a polynomial of degree 14 to approximate R The maximum error\n75\t *    of this polynomial approximation is bounded by 2**-58.45. In\n76\t *    other words,\n77\t *                2      4      6      8      10      12      14\n78\t *        R(z) ~ Lp1*s +Lp2*s +Lp3*s +Lp4*s +Lp5*s  +Lp6*s  +Lp7*s\n79\t *      (the values of Lp1 to Lp7 are listed in the program)\n80\t *    and\n81\t *        |      2          14          |     -58.45\n82\t *        | Lp1*s +...+Lp7*s    -  R(z) | <= 2\n83\t *        |                             |\n84\t *    Note that 2s = f - s*f = f - hfsq + s*hfsq, where hfsq = f*f/2.\n85\t *    In order to guarantee error in log below 1ulp, we compute log\n86\t *    by\n87\t *        log1p(f) = f - (hfsq - s*(hfsq+R)).\n88\t *\n89\t *    3. Finally, log1p(x) = k*ln2 + log1p(f).\n90\t *                  = k*ln2_hi+(f-(hfsq-(s*(hfsq+R)+k*ln2_lo)))\n91\t *       Here ln2 is split into two floating point number:\n92\t *     